WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:06.040
Arrival is a classily crafted slice of sci-fi
with a sublime performance by Amy Adams at

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.040
its core.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.580
But how did the filmmakers actually put the
film together?

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.180
In this video, I'm going to take a look at
seven fascinating secrets you need to know

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.050
about the making of the movie.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:19.270
The heptapod's spaceship owes its design to
an asteroid named after the Greek goddess

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:21.470
of law and legislation, Eunomia!

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:25.680
During research for the design of the alien
craft, director Denis Villeneuve came across

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.430
the asteroid 15 Eunomia and was attracted
by what he called its "insane shape, like

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:35.070
a strange egg" and thought that kind of pebble
or oval shape would bring a perfect sense

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:40.260
of menace and mystery to the heptapod's spacecraft
which, in the film, is made from materials

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:45.051
unknown to man and defies current human scientific
understanding in terms of how it works and

00:00:45.051 --> 00:00:46.570
travels through space.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:51.300
Octopuses, whales, elephants, and even spiders
were all sources of inspiration when it came

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.829
to creating the aliens, Abbott and Costello.

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:58.399
Villeneuve wanted their design to evoke a
very strong presence, an air of intelligence,

00:00:58.399 --> 00:01:02.350
and the feeling of being close to a "huge
beast underwater".

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.329
And he wanted to bring to life the sort of
surreal creature you might imagine in a dream

00:01:06.329 --> 00:01:10.259
or a nightmare and, in the later stages of
the film, he wanted them to be suggestive

00:01:10.259 --> 00:01:11.789
of the Grim Reaper.

00:01:11.789 --> 00:01:15.979
On top of that, it was important to Villeneuve
that the reveal of the full alien body happened

00:01:15.979 --> 00:01:20.539
slowly, over the course of the film, in order
to build the audience's suspense and intrigue,

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:25.549
and he also wanted to contrast their quiet
arrival and calm communication with Louise

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:29.840
and Ian inside the spaceship with the panicked
reaction of the world outside.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.039
To achieve this, he brought on board creature
concept artist Carlos Huante, who worked on

00:01:34.039 --> 00:01:36.020
Ridley Scott's Prometheus.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:40.079
The underwater creatures like octopuses and
squid, which so influenced the heptapods'

00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:44.999
appearance, also inspired the filmmakers to
use blasts of ink to form their language.

00:01:44.999 --> 00:01:49.499
While the idea that the heptapods' non-linear
language should consist of circles marked

00:01:49.499 --> 00:01:54.420
with twists and curls came from screenwriter
Eric Heisserer, who even included rough sketches

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:55.729
of them in his script.

00:01:55.729 --> 00:02:00.080
The alien's language is an absolutely crucial
part of the film and Villeneuve wanted it

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:05.599
to be striking, almost frightening, completely
distinct from human language, and suggestive

00:02:05.599 --> 00:02:06.649
of a different mind-set.

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:11.780
So, Heisserer's concept was further developed
by production designer Patrice Vermette working

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.180
with artist Martine Bertrand, whose idea it
was for the language to appear as abstract

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:18.530
inky circles that resembled coffee s.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:23.130
Vermette and his team then immersed themselves
in the design process of creating a fictional

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:28.030
language that had its own set of rules and
was internally accurate, not just a randomly

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.060
assembled bunch of circles, splotches and
flourishes.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:36.280
In fact, they created their own dictionary
with over 100 logograms, each of which conveys

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:41.099
full sentences or paragraphs, and 71 of those
were used in the film.

00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:45.500
"Dirty sci-fi" is what Villeneuve and his
cinematographer Bradford Young call the look

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.580
they created together for Arrival.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:52.620
Basically, they wanted to avoid the kind of
spectacle you see in a typical sci-fi blockbuster

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.450
and instead go for something more delicate
and light.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:03:00.520
What that means for the film is that it feels
like "this was happening on a bad Tuesday

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.280
morning," to quote Villeneuve, "[�] like
when you were a kid on the school bus on a

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.090
rainy day and you'd dream while looking [out
the window] at the clouds � that kind of

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:08.090
atmosphere".

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:12.580
And a major influence on Arrival's very raw
and naturalistic look for cinematographer

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:17.659
Bradford Young was the work of Scandinavian
photographer Martina Hoogland Ivanow, especially

00:03:17.659 --> 00:03:21.379
the subdued and mysterious quality of her
exhibition and book 'Speedway'.

00:03:21.379 --> 00:03:26.900
The film we see today is very different than
it was originally going to be as its whole

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.560
structure was changed in post-production.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.609
According to Villeneuve, while the original
screenplay made sense on paper, the visual

00:03:33.609 --> 00:03:38.889
medium of cinema made telling a non-linear
story with a twist like Arrival much more

00:03:38.889 --> 00:03:39.889
challenging.

00:03:39.889 --> 00:03:43.010
And when the director got into the editing
room, he realised that if he told the film

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:47.621
exactly as it was in the script, its structure
wouldn't be dynamic enough, it would feel

00:03:47.621 --> 00:03:51.880
repetitive in places, and there would be some
discrepancies within the characters.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:56.299
Fortunately, Arrival's editor Joe Walker came
to the rescue and suggested restructuring

00:03:56.299 --> 00:04:00.830
the footage they'd shot in a less linear fashion,
which also means that the very structure of

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:04.029
the film now reflects some of the movie's
main themes.

00:04:04.029 --> 00:04:08.590
One of the only Hollywood Studios that showed
any interest in making Arrival when the script

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:13.260
was initially pitched to them, actually wanted
a male protagonist to replace the film's central

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:17.600
character, linguist Dr Louise Banks, who,
by the way, is also the lead in the short

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:18.910
story the film's based on.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:23.600
However, the movie's producers stuck to their
guns and financed the film independently,

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.240
and later with Amy Adams attached, they sold
the rights to distribute the film in North

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:33.620
America to Paramount for a record sum of $20
million at Cannes Film Festival in 2014.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:37.650
Interestingly enough, before the role of Dr
Banks in Arrival came along, Adams had been

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.630
planning to take some time away from making
movies and just hang out with her family,

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:45.240
however, when she read the screenplay, she
fell in love with the story, and the opportunity

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.650
to work with Denis Villeneuve was also too
much of a draw to turn it down.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:54.199
Originally, the film was named after Ted Chiang's
short story, but director Denis Villeneuve

00:04:54.199 --> 00:04:58.480
decided to change the film's title from Story
of Your Life because he felt audiences would

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.400
think the film was a rom-com rather than a
sci-fi movie, and because he believed they'd

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.490
moved away from the short story's focus.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:09.180
Indeed, to increase the film's drama and tension,
the screenplay made some significant changes

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:14.050
to the original tale, for example, the script
added global conflict by having the aliens

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:18.900
arrive in a dozen locations on Earth, which
in turn kicked off a ticking-clock scenario.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:23.500
In the end, after considering hundreds of
potential titles, the filmmakers settled on

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:25.789
the very first title they thought of: "Arrival".

